--- MESSAGE ---
case is reserved, and TypeName.equals and RootInterface are the first two case-branches

The construct was specified at 94b35cc, the kind and the engine's support for it landed at 606c1a6
with nothing authoring it. This reserves the word and converts the only two definitions in any
corpus that carried it inside host-opaque text — found by `grep -w case` over every .drake and
.json, not by memory — so the tree never sits with the token reserved and still opaque somewhere.

TYPENAME.EQUALS — THE FIRST MATCH THE ENGINE WRITES FROM A DEFINITION

    dyn equals Boolean [
      parameters
        par other Any
      case tn TypeName [
        = name == tn.name && namePackage == tn.namePackage && typeParameters == tn.typeParameters
      ]
      case [
        = false
      ]
    ]

A result-shaped run: each branch carries a value and the dyn carries none, so the dispatch IS the
method's result. The default branch is authored because a result run owes one — exhaustiveness is
the author's there — and the scrutinee is not, because a dyn with cases has exactly one parameter
and that is it. The branch values stay strings: `==` and `&&` are not declared operators, and
authoring them as trees would have put in the JSON what Drake cannot parse back (the rule from
8239d08). The surface-loss count does not move.

ROOTINTERFACE — THE SITE THAT MOTIVATED THE CONSTRUCT

    message
      case a AlarmSignal [
        mon session.insert parameters par Seq(a.infochemical): _*
      ]
      case d DroughtCue [
        mon session.insert parameters par Seq(d.infochemical): _*
      ]
      mon session.fire parameters

An effect-shaped run, followed by a statement that belongs to the enclosing block — the exact case
that made a case-branch bracket itself. No default is authored; the target writes `case _ => ()`,
because a message matching no branch is a no-op and the projection owes the catch-all. `msg` is
the projection's name for the scrutinee, not the definition's. The single-line Scala `match` that
ScenarioDrakeTest's own header called "smuggled through as host-opaque text — it will parse, and
that is precisely the problem" is gone.

THE GAP 3a COULD NOT SEE

`Generator.targetBody` — the entry normalization that rebuilds every element to rewrite its type
expressions — matched over the kinds with no Case arm. A sealed-match gap is a warning, so 606c1a6
compiled; the first definition carrying a Case crashed it. Added, with the note that this is the
class of gap an increment that authors nothing cannot exercise, which is why 3b exists.

WHAT IS PREDICTED

The two .scala files were spliced by hand from caseDispatch's rules, because the crashed generator
cannot be recompiled from the session. The drakes are not predicted — they are Drake.emit output.
The gates that check the predictions are named in the record header.

